on the other axis we have data  literature  um of data literacy excuse me so again  we have these three pillars here first  one is data leadership one of the  biggest uh factors that we found to be  either success or or not success when it  comes to implementing a larger  data-driven policy is is your leadership  uh bought in  can they allocate the resources can they  allocate the time do they understand  what it means to be data driven make  sure your leadership is on board data  governance is another big thing  how many of your organizations have data  governance have talked about data  governance or implementing data  governance  you oh I would I would get on that I  think that's that's a really important  component because data governance  essentially outlines the guidelines for  how to use data responsibly for example  there's a ton of dark data that exists  because people have a bunch of those  Excel spreadsheets downloaded to their  computer and not anywhere else right so  what happens when they leave and their  laptop gets wiped all of that data goes  down the drain  these simple guidelines of where to  store your data how to label your  columns how to handle data appropriately  minor guidelines that make a really  really big difference and last but not  least data knowledge does your staff  understand  um how to ask the right questions of  data one of the biggest things that we  see  if you're a data professional is they  get a lot of queries from their  colleagues about SQL queries  um about questions that are business  related but they're not really expanding  on what they really need and so there  are again these miscommunications  if your colleagues around you understand  how to use data how to ask questions  they feel empowered they can actually do  some of that work themselves and then  that leaves you to really dig into more  in-depth uh you know algorithms and  modeling and things like that  okay  I have four minutes left I think we're  good actually  how can you encourage data-driven  thinking so we've been doing this for  over eight years  um we've seen a lot of ways for this to  be implemented successfully  and it starts with you all who are in  the room  so the first thing that we like to say  is to be the role model yourselves  sometimes that sounds really daunting  but you can take one step at a time so  what does that really look like in  practice right  um if you are running a project or you  have colleagues that are doing an  analysis ask for those metrics  and show them what you mean if you if  you have a prototype there you can also  ask questions about the data dig into it  do people account for those biases where  is the data coming from are those models  valid have they been tested with real  world data  use tools and exercises to get people  out of the box  um demonstrate your decision making  during meetings so especially if you're  again in that leadership like literally  point to the chart that you are using to  inform your decision right be very  deliberate about that and start to show  people what that means to be data driven  and engage in creative thinking to drive  Innovation so one of the events that we  love to see are hackathons or lunch and  learns and I think I'm eating into my  next slide maybe a little bit  um so find ways to showcase the good  work that people are doing at the same  time  um provide people a safe space one of  the things that we see most often is  people are afraid to fail  they're like I'm gonna do this model and  if it doesn't turn out exactly right I'm  not going to be allowed to do anything  else  I think everyone in this room  understands the first time you run a  model  it's not going to be perfect right we  have to iterate sometimes we need to go  back and get more data or we need to  redefine the question itself be a safe  space for other people  let people explore their ideas help them  talk it through and provide them a place  to fail because that's how they learn  from it right and that's the best way to  to do any of these projects in in my  opinion is to be able to really learn  from our mistakes and if you provide  that safe space people are more likely  to take that risk themselves  so that's really important to do as well  um and last but not least is give  recognition people love to be recognized  and one of the biggest drivers that  we've seen to help Inspire others is  showing successful projects within an  organization so let's say you're on a  team that did great work maybe they  implemented a model they were able to  automate some processes put on a lunch  and learn you know put it in a  newsletter do a hackathon around the  next stage of it shout it from the  rooftops because other teams will see  that and they'll say oh you did this  within the restrictions that we have on  our networks you did this within  um our organization's Mission and I  actually think I have a similar problem  that we can solve right and and it's  really fun for the people who are doing  the hard work frankly you know it's  important to give recognition when when  that good work is done and also when  there are improvements that need to be  made  so  this was a very quick talk I have one  minute left so I think I I did really  well on timing  um but hopefully you're able to take  away some of these action items maybe  pick one that you think is that low  hanging fruit that you can go back to  your team with next week and just say  hey let's start using charts uh let's  start using the dashboards that we've  created to actually drive some of those  decisions that we're discussing  with that I'm going to finish out my  talk just to say if you want to learn  more about us you can go to data Society  you can also just email me I didn't  include my last name because nobody  would ever email me correctly so it's  just murav datasociety.com sure thank  you all so much
 

